{"kind": "functional", "gpus": 2, "run": 2, "case": 6, "expected": "__exit__", "answer": "# Python Context Managers: A Detailed Explanation\n\n## What Is a Context Manager?\n\nA **context manager** is a Python object that defines the runtime context for a block of code, typically using the `with` statement. It ensures that resources are properly acquired before entering the block and released after exiting the block — even if an exception occurs.\n\nThe most common use case is **resource management**: files, database connections, locks, network sockets, etc.\n\n---\n\n## The `with` Statement\n\n```python\nwith expression as variable:\n    # code block\n```\n\n- `expression` evaluates to a **context manager object**.\n- `variable` (optional) receives the return value of `__enter__`.\n- The code block runs within the context.\n- Upon exit (normal or exceptional), cleanup happens automatically.\n\n---\n\n## The Protocol: `__enter__` and `__exit__`\n\nA context manager must implement two methods:\n\n### 1. `__enter__(self)`\n\n- Called when entering the `with` block.\n- Returns the value that will be bound to the `as` variable.\n- Typically acquires resources (opens a file, locks a mutex, etc.).\n\n### ", "passed": true, "ttft_seconds": 0.5440491065382957, "elapsed_seconds": 7.686065152287483, "timings": {"cache_n": 0, "prompt_n": 29, "prompt_ms": 499.8, "prompt_per_token_ms": 17.234, "prompt_per_second": 58.0, "predicted_n": 256, "predicted_ms": 7185.7, "predicted_per_token_ms": 28.069, "predicted_per_second": 35.6, "draft_n": 276, "draft_n_accepted": 163}, "engine": {"generated": 256, "prompt_tokens": 29, "prompt_ms": 499.8, "decode_ms": 7185.7, "finish": "length", "drafts_accepted": 163, "drafts_offered": 276, "reused": 0, "hits": 175123, "lookups": 177120, "ram_blobs": 1387, "file_blobs": 0, "file_mb": 0.0, "prompt_read": 29, "offloaded": 0, "chunk": 0, "experts_streamed": 0}, "mtp": true, "max_drafts": 3, "draft_acceptance": 0.5905797101449275, "layout": "layer"}
